ms no leftover failed services, no unexpected listening sockets, and no package-health regressions after the executor's work; the only after_executor changes are the expected boot-id/tmp-dir churn from the reboot. The .bak backup files for pwquality.conf and common-password were retained, which is standard defensive practice and not unwanted residue. No unnecessary or unrelated mutations were observed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3aff5132827046b1\",\"cmd-a4261e32e23e4637\",\"cmd-eaa76878fb1a4b47\",\"cmd-05960f9ac4b846ef\",\"cmd-2d996d8c298d42fd\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly installed and configured pam_pwquality on node1 (minlen=12, minclass=3), and directly demonstrated via live passwd attempts that short passwords and passwords with fewer than 3 character classes are rejected while compliant passwords are accepted, both before and after a full node restart with a confirmed boot-identity change. Configuration and enforcement were shown to persist across the reboot. Operational hygiene was clean: all mutations (package install, config edits with backups, test-user creation/deletion, reboot) were purposeful and bounded, with no leftover residue or broken state.\",\"limitations\":[\"Testing was limited to a single non-root PAM-managed user (pwtestuser/pwtestuser2) via the passwd command; explicit verification against a completely different account-creation path (e.g. useradd with an initial password set via chpasswd) was not separately exercised, though chpasswd itself is not PAM-gated so this is expected and not a gap in the policy.\",\"enforce_for_root was added to pwquality.conf, which is a reasonable but not explicitly requested extension beyond 'new user passwords'; it does not conflict with the task and was not separately tested against root.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-minlen-1